1. Executive Summary
This document presents a Proof of Concept (PoC) for migrating DBT (Data Build Tool) execution from ECS on EC2 to Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate as the compute layer. The PoC was completed successfully, with all validation tests passing.
The current EC2-based ECS cluster requires continuous infrastructure management, capacity planning, and lifecycle maintenance — responsibilities that add significant overhead to the platform team and introduce availability risk. The proposed architecture removes EC2 compute entirely. DBT tasks are executed as short-lived, serverless Fargate tasks, fully isolated per tenant, and billed only for the duration of each run.
The change aligns the platform with a multi-tenant service model where each tenant receives dedicated compute, IAM roles, a Step Functions state machine, and a Service Catalog product — ensuring true isolation with zero shared infrastructure at runtime.
Key Outcomes of the PoC:
	∙	DBT execution validated end-to-end on Fargate
	∙	Task lifecycle verified: START → RUNNING → STOPPED
	∙	CloudWatch logs confirmed streaming correctly
	∙	S3 outputs generated successfully
	∙	IAM permissions validated across all roles
	∙	Zero EC2 capacity or instance-related issues observed

2. Problem Statement — Current State
The existing DBT platform runs ECS tasks on EC2 instances managed within an Auto Scaling Group. While functional, this approach introduces a class of operational problems that are disproportionate to the actual workload being executed — short-lived, batch-oriented data transformation jobs.
Operational Challenges with EC2-Based ECS:
	∙	AMI lifecycle management: EC2 instances require regular AMI refreshes to incorporate OS patches, security updates, and dependency changes.
	∙	Auto Scaling Group complexity: ASG configuration, warm pools, scale-in/scale-out policies, and launch templates require ongoing tuning.
	∙	Capacity planning: The cluster must be pre-sized for peak load, resulting in idle EC2 capacity during off-peak periods — wasted spend.
	∙	Instance health and failure handling: EC2 instance failures require replacement, draining, and re-registration with the ECS cluster.
	∙	Multi-tenant resource contention: Shared EC2 instances across tenant workloads introduce noisy-neighbor risk and reduce isolation guarantees.
	∙	Security exposure: A shared host surface across tenants increases the blast radius of any container escape or privilege escalation.
	∙	Operational overhead: The platform team must monitor EC2 health, manage instance retirement, and respond to capacity-related incidents.
Core Issue: The EC2 layer provides no architectural value for a short-lived batch workload like DBT. It exists purely as a scheduling substrate — and that substrate can be replaced with AWS-managed Fargate compute, eliminating the entire class of EC2-related problems.

3. What Is AWS Fargate?
Amazon ECS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers. With Fargate, teams define what the container needs — CPU, memory, networking configuration, and IAM role — and AWS provisions, manages, and retires the underlying compute infrastructure transparently.
Unlike ECS on EC2, there are no instances to patch, no AMIs to maintain, no Auto Scaling Groups to configure, and no host-level access to manage. Each Fargate task runs in its own isolated environment with a dedicated Elastic Network Interface (ENI), its own CPU and memory allocation, and an AWS-managed runtime. When the task completes, the compute is released and billing stops.
Why Fargate Is the Right Fit for DBT:
	∙	DBT runs are short-lived and batch-oriented — exactly the pattern Fargate is optimised for.
	∙	There is no persistent state on the compute layer; all outputs go to S3 and CloudWatch.
	∙	DBT workloads do not require specialised host capabilities (GPU, custom kernel modules, etc.).
	∙	Tenants are isolated by definition — Fargate’s per-task model maps directly to the multi-tenant architecture.
	∙	Fargate integrates natively with Step Functions, IAM, CloudWatch, ECR, and VPC networking.

4. Architecture Overview
4.1 Resources Created
The following AWS resources are provisioned as part of this architecture. Each tenant receives its own dedicated set of resources, ensuring complete isolation at both the control plane and data plane levels.



|Resource                        |Purpose                                                                                  |
|--------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|SM IAM Role                     |Step Functions execution role — grants ecs:RunTask and iam:PassRole permissions          |
|ECS Task IAM Role               |Runtime role assumed by the Fargate task — scoped to S3, CloudWatch, and tenant resources|
|ECS Execution Role              |Allows Fargate to pull the container image from ECR and stream logs to CloudWatch        |
|ECS Log Group                   |CloudWatch Log Group for capturing DBT execution output per tenant                       |
|ECS Cluster                     |Shared logical ECS cluster — no EC2 instances attached                                   |
|Task Definition                 |Defines container image, CPU/memory, network mode, roles, and environment per tenant     |
|Task Definition SSM Parameter   |Stores the task definition ARN in SSM for Service Catalog product consumption            |
|ECS-TASK Service Catalog Product|Tenant-facing product that accepts parameters and triggers the ECS Fargate DBT run       |
|Step Functions State Machine    |Orchestrates the DBT execution flow — invokes ECS RunTask and handles state transitions  |
|ECR Repository (Local)          |Tenant-specific registry for the synced DBT container image                              |

4.2 Runtime Flow



|Step|Actor               |Action                                                                                                    |
|----|--------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|1   |Service Catalog     |Tenant invokes the ECS-TASK Service Catalog product with parameters: TaskDefinitionSSM and SynchNexusImage|
|2   |Step Functions      |Service Catalog triggers the Step Functions State Machine using the SM IAM Role                           |
|3   |Step Functions → ECS|State Machine calls ecs:RunTask, passing task definition and network config (subnets, security groups)    |
|4   |ECS Control Plane   |ECS validates the Task Definition and selects Fargate as the launch type                                  |
|5   |Fargate             |AWS provisions an isolated Fargate task: dedicated ENI, isolated CPU/memory, AWS-managed runtime          |
|6   |Execution Role      |Fargate assumes the Execution Role to pull the DBT container image from the tenant ECR repository         |
|7   |DBT Container       |Container starts, DBT execution begins — transformation jobs run against the target data warehouse        |
|8   |CloudWatch          |Fargate streams container logs to the tenant CloudWatch Log Group in real time                            |
|9   |S3                  |DBT outputs (artifacts, run results) are written to S3 as configured                                      |
|10  |Task Completion     |Fargate task transitions to STOPPED — compute is released and billing ceases                              |

