6. Multi-Tenancy Architecture
One of the most significant architectural advantages of the Fargate-based approach is its alignment with a true multi-tenant service model. Each tenant on the platform receives a completely independent set of resources — from the Service Catalog product entry point all the way down to the runtime Fargate task. There is no shared infrastructure at any layer during execution.
6.1 Per-Tenant Resource Isolation



|Component              |Per-Tenant Isolation                                          |Benefit                                                      |
|-----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
|Service Catalog Product|Each tenant has a dedicated ECS-TASK Service Catalog product  |Tenant-specific configuration with no shared state           |
|IAM Role (ECS Task)    |Unique ECS Task IAM Role per tenant                           |Least-privilege access scoped to tenant resources only       |
|IAM Role (SM)          |Unique Step Functions IAM Role per tenant                     |Prevents cross-tenant state machine execution                |
|State Machine          |Dedicated Step Functions state machine per tenant             |Independent orchestration, no contention or bleed-over       |
|Task Definition        |Tenant-specific ECS Task Definition (image, CPU, memory)      |Right-sized compute and isolated container runtime per tenant|
|Fargate Task (Runtime) |Each run spawns a fully isolated Fargate task with its own ENI|Zero cross-tenant resource sharing at runtime                |
|CloudWatch Log Group   |Separate log groups per tenant                                |Audit isolation; tenants cannot access each other’s logs     |
|ECR Image              |Tenant image synced via Nexus to tenant ECR repository        |Image supply chain isolated per tenant                       |

This model ensures that a misconfiguration, failure, or resource spike in one tenant’s DBT run has zero impact on any other tenant.
6.2 Elimination of EC2 Compute Waste in Multi-Tenant Context
In the previous EC2-based architecture, all tenants’ tasks competed for a shared pool of EC2 instances. This introduced several structural inefficiencies:
	∙	Idle Compute Waste: EC2 instances ran continuously even during periods with no active tenant DBT runs. Every idle minute represented sunk cost with no associated workload.
	∙	Over-Provisioning Requirement: To guarantee availability for all tenants during peak periods, the cluster had to be sized for worst-case concurrent load — meaning average utilisation was far below 100%.
	∙	Noisy Neighbour Risk: A resource-intensive DBT run from one tenant could degrade scheduling performance for other tenants competing for slots on shared EC2 instances.
	∙	Uneven Tenant Footprint: Tenants with large DBT models consumed disproportionate EC2 resources, creating invisible cost allocation challenges.
With Fargate, each tenant’s task provisions its own isolated compute only for the duration of the run. The moment the DBT job completes, the compute is released. There is no cluster to maintain, no idle instances, and no resource contention between tenants — by architectural design.
Multi-Tenancy Principle: With Fargate, the platform moves from a shared-compute model (EC2 pool) to a dedicated-compute-per-execution model. Every DBT run — for every tenant — is fully isolated, independently billed, and operationally decoupled from all other tenants.

7. Fargate: Advantages & Trade-offs
Advantages
	∙	Zero EC2 infrastructure to manage — no AMI patches, ASG tuning, or instance lifecycle
	∙	Per-task billing ensures cost matches actual execution — no idle EC2 costs
	∙	Native task-level isolation: dedicated ENI, CPU, and memory per run
	∙	Scales automatically — no pre-warming or capacity reservation needed
	∙	Ideal for short-lived, batch-oriented workloads like DBT transformations
	∙	Stronger security posture — no shared host between tenant workloads
	∙	Simplified operations — teams focus on container definitions, not servers
	∙	Consistent runtime environment — no host-level drift or config divergence
	∙	Works natively with existing CloudWatch, S3, and IAM integrations
Limitations / Trade-offs
	∙	Slightly longer cold start vs warm EC2 cluster (~10–30s per task launch)
	∙	No access to EC2 host-level tooling (custom kernel modules, GPU access)
	∙	Per-task pricing can theoretically exceed reserved EC2 cost at extreme sustained load — not applicable to the DBT batch pattern
	∙	Does not support all instance families or custom AMI configurations
	∙	Requires awsvpc network mode — cannot use bridge or host networking
	∙	Task size is bounded by defined CPU/memory tiers — no burst beyond task limits
Assessment: The trade-offs are either negligible or acceptable for the DBT batch execution pattern. DBT runs are not latency-sensitive, and cold start times of 10–30 seconds are immaterial against typical DBT execution durations of several minutes. The advantages — zero EC2 management, per-task isolation, and cost alignment — are architecturally transformative for a multi-tenant platform.

