11. Security Considerations
11.1 Improvements Over EC2 Model
	∙	No shared host: Fargate eliminates the shared EC2 host surface. A compromised container cannot pivot to another tenant’s workload via host-level access.
	∙	Immutable runtime: Fargate’s managed compute environment cannot be modified by tenant workloads — no writable host filesystem, no persistent SSH access.
	∙	Per-task IAM roles: Each tenant’s task assumes a scoped IAM role with least-privilege permissions. There is no IAM role inheritance from shared EC2 instance profiles.
	∙	Network isolation: The dedicated ENI per task, combined with tenant-scoped security groups, ensures network traffic cannot cross tenant boundaries.
11.2 IAM Role Structure
	∙	SM IAM Role: Grants ecs:RunTask and iam:PassRole only — scoped to the tenant’s task definition and ECS Task IAM Role ARN.
	∙	ECS Task IAM Role: Runtime permissions for the DBT container — S3 read/write to tenant buckets, CloudWatch log publishing, and data warehouse credentials via SSM/Secrets Manager.
	∙	ECS Execution Role: Restricted to ecr:GetAuthorizationToken, ecr:BatchGetImage, and logs:CreateLogStream — no access to tenant data resources.
11.3 Recommendations
	∙	Enable VPC Flow Logs on the subnets used by Fargate tasks for a network audit trail.
	∙	Apply SCPs (Service Control Policies) at the account level to prevent Fargate tasks from assuming roles outside the tenant boundary.
	∙	Use AWS Secrets Manager for DBT profile credentials rather than environment variables in task definitions.
	∙	Implement CloudTrail logging for ecs:RunTask events to maintain an audit log of all DBT execution invocations.

12. Risks & Mitigations



|Risk                                                                    |Severity|Mitigation                                                                                                                                       |
|------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Fargate cold start adds latency to DBT run initiation                   |Low     |DBT runs are batch/non-latency-sensitive. 10–30s startup is immaterial vs typical execution duration of minutes.                                 |
|Fargate task size limits may be insufficient for very large DBT models  |Medium  |Fargate supports up to 16 vCPU and 120GB memory per task — sufficient for all current and projected workloads. Monitor and right-size per tenant.|
|Fargate Spot interruptions if Spot is used for cost optimisation        |Medium  |Use Fargate Spot only for non-critical runs. Configure Step Functions retry logic to handle interruptions gracefully.                            |
|Increased per-execution cost vs reserved EC2 at very high sustained load|Low     |Not applicable to DBT batch pattern. If continuous 24/7 load emerges, re-evaluate — but this is not the current or projected usage pattern.      |
|ECR image pull failure if Execution Role permissions are incorrect      |Low     |Validated during PoC. IAM policies templated and tested. Include ECR pull test in CI pipeline for task definition changes.                       |
|Network connectivity issues (NAT Gateway, VPC endpoints)                |Low     |Tested during PoC. VPC routing confirmed. Recommend VPC endpoints for ECR and S3 to eliminate NAT Gateway dependency.                            |

13. Recommended Migration Plan
Phase 1 — Foundation (Week 1–2)
	∙	Finalise Fargate task definition template for all active tenants.
	∙	Update Service Catalog product to deploy Fargate-compatible task definitions.
	∙	Validate VPC endpoints for ECR and S3 to eliminate NAT Gateway dependency.
	∙	Update IAM role templates (SM IAM Role, ECS Task IAM Role, Execution Role) across all tenants.
Phase 2 — Parallel Running (Week 3–4)
	∙	Run DBT jobs on both EC2-based and Fargate-based paths in parallel for selected tenants.
	∙	Compare output correctness, execution time, and log completeness between the two paths.
	∙	Monitor CloudWatch metrics: task launch time, execution duration, failure rate.
Phase 3 — Full Cutover (Week 5–6)
	∙	Migrate all tenants to Fargate launch type.
	∙	Decommission EC2 instances and Auto Scaling Group from the ECS cluster.
	∙	Remove EC2-related infrastructure (launch templates, AMIs, ASG policies).
	∙	Update runbooks and operational documentation to reflect the Fargate model.
Phase 4 — Optimisation (Post-Migration)
	∙	Implement Fargate Spot for non-critical or dev/test tenant runs.
	∙	Add per-tenant cost tagging for chargeback reporting.
	∙	Review and right-size CPU/memory allocations per tenant task definition.
	∙	Implement Step Functions retry and error-handling logic for resilience.

14. Benefits Summary
Zero EC2 Management
No AMI lifecycle, no Auto Scaling Group management, no EC2 patching, no instance health monitoring. The entire class of EC2 operational work is eliminated.
True Multi-Tenant Isolation
Every tenant receives dedicated IAM roles, a State Machine, a Service Catalog product, and a fully isolated Fargate task at runtime. Zero shared compute across tenants.
Cost Alignment
Billing matches actual execution. No idle EC2 costs. Each tenant’s DBT run is independently billed per second of vCPU and memory consumed. Right-sizing is per-tenant, not cluster-wide.
Operational Simplicity
Platform team focuses on container and task definition management only. No infrastructure layer to operate, monitor, or recover from failures.
Improved Security Posture
No shared host surface. Per-task IAM roles. Dedicated ENI per execution. Immutable runtime environment. Significantly reduced blast radius.
Platform Scalability
Adding a new tenant requires deploying a new Service Catalog product, IAM roles, and task definition — no cluster resizing, no EC2 capacity planning required.

15. Recommendation & Next Steps
Based on the successful completion of this PoC and the architectural analysis presented in this document, the recommendation is to proceed with the full migration of DBT execution from ECS on EC2 to ECS on Fargate.
The Fargate model is architecturally superior for this workload in every dimension that matters for a multi-tenant batch platform: isolation, cost, operational overhead, security, and scalability. The only trade-off identified (cold start time) is immaterial for the DBT batch execution pattern.
